 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot K \cdot C)$ per iteration, where $N$ is the number of rays in the batch, $K$ is the number of samples per ray, and $C$ is the cost of the neural network forward pass [S5, S11, unofficial]. Total training time scales linearly with the number of iterations (typically 100k-1M) [S11, S15, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot K)$ to store intermediate activations for backpropagation if using standard reverse-mode AD [S5, S11, unofficial]. Chunking rays reduces peak VRAM usage [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On Blender synthetic data (800x800), training takes several hours to days depending on hardware and batch size [S5, S11, unofficial]. PSNR typically converges to ~30-35 dB for simple scenes [S12].
-*   **Scaling:** Scales poorly with scene size due to the dense MLP evaluation [S16, unofficial]. Acceleration methods (hashing, grids) are required for large scenes [S7, S16, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable end-to-end, produces high-quality novel views [S5, S11, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Slow training and rendering, prone to overfitting on sparse views, requires careful hyperparameter tuning [S1][S18, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When high-quality novel view synthesis is required and computational resources are sufficient.
-*   **When not to use:** For real-time applications or very large scenes without acceleration structures [S16, S18, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot K)$, where $N_{iters}$ is iterations, $N_{rand}$ is batch size, and $K$ is samples per ray [S5, S11, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N_{rand} \cdot K)$ for the forward pass, plus $O(\Theta)$ for network weights [S5, S16, unofficial]. VRAM is limited by chunking rays [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance**: Standard NeRF trains for ~100,000 iterations on Blender datasets [S11, unofficial]. On LLFF with 3 views, using 50 target rays yields PSNR 20.33, SSIM 0.661, LPIPS 0.276; increasing to 200 rays degrades performance [S21].
+*   **Scaling**: Batch size trade-off: larger batches (e.g., $2^{14}$) hinder data loading and GPU occupancy, while smaller batches ($2^{10}$) cap iterations per second [S16, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Simple to implement, differentiable, produces high-quality novel views [S5, S11, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: Slow convergence, high memory usage for large scenes, prone to artifacts (floaters) in sparse views [S1][S18, unofficial].
+*   **When to use**: Standard multi-view reconstruction with dense input views [S11, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use**: Sparse views (use [Sparse View and Initialization Methods](9394-sparse-view-and-initialization-methods.md)), real-time rendering (use [Acceleration and Sparse Structures](11405-acceleration-and-sparse-structures.md)), or dynamic scenes without temporal priors [S18, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The NeRF training loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S5, S11, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of coordinate-based neural networks for implicit representations [S3, unofficial]. The method uses stochastic gradient descent with Adam optimizer and positional encoding to learn the radiance field [S5, S11, unofficial]. It has become the standard baseline for neural rendering research [S25].
+The NeRF training loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S5, S11, unofficial]. It builds on the differentiable volume rendering equation [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. The method uses coordinate-based MLPs with positional encoding [S11, unofficial]. It is widely used in computer vision for novel view synthesis, 3D reconstruction, and editing [S1, S28].
 
